.TH SSHMGR 1 "2026-09-30" "sshmgr 2.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sshmgr \- connect to ssh hosts from a menu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshmgr
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshmgr
keeps a list of ssh hosts in a json file and connects to them from a menu
instead of from remembered command lines. Run it without options to pick a
host with fuzzy search; the preview shows whether the host answers a ping and
which user, port and bastion host will be used.
.PP
Hosts can also be added and removed from inside
.BR sshmgr ,
without editing the json by hand, and connections can optionally go through
kitty's ssh kitten.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B sshmgr
.br
Open the host menu. Type to filter, arrow keys to move,
.B enter
to connect,
.B ctrl\-q
to quit. If a host has a
.B jumphost
it is connected through it with
.BR "ssh \-J" .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-edit
Open the hosts file in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add
Add a host. Without arguments a form is opened, see
.BR "THE ADD FORM" .
.TP
.BI \-a " NAME HOST [USER [PORT [JUMPHOST]]]"
Add a host without asking anything. Empty arguments are allowed and are left
out of the entry, so an empty port keeps the default of 22. Fails if the name
is already taken or the port is not a number between 1 and 65535.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remove
Remove hosts. Without arguments they are picked with
.BR fzf ,
using the same preview as the menu.
.TP
.BI \-r " NAME..."
Remove the given hosts without asking anything. Nothing is removed unless
every name exists.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ping
Ping all known hosts in parallel with
.BR fping .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.SH THE ADD FORM
Run as
.B sshmgr \-a
with a terminal, sshmgr draws its own form: one text box per field with a
caret in the field being edited. Anything that keeps the entry from being
written is shown on the field that causes it, and the entry is only written
once it is complete.
.PP
Keys:
.TP
.B tab, enter
Next field.
.TP
.B shift\-tab, up
Previous field.
.TP
.B left, right
Move the caret inside the field.
.TP
.B backspace, delete
Delete a character.
.TP
.B ctrl\-u
.br
Clear the whole field.
.TP
.B ctrl\-s, ctrl-d
Write the entry.
.TP
.B esc, ctrl\-c
Quit without writing.
.PP
The form needs a terminal. Without one, for example in a script or with
output redirected, sshmgr asks for each field on stdin instead.
.SH THE REMOVE LIST
.TP
.B tab
.br
Select the entry and move on.
.TP
.B enter
.br
Remove everything selected.
.TP
.B esc
.br
Quit.
.PP
Nothing is removed while browsing: the file is only changed when
.B enter
is pressed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The hosts live in
.IR ~/.config/sshmgr/known_hosts.json ,
which is created with an empty list on first run and validated with
.B jq
on every start.
.PP
.nf
{
  "kitten_ssh": false,
  "hosts": [
    { "name": "web-server", "host": "example.com", "user": "deploy", "port": "2222" },
    { "name": "internal-db", "host": "10.0.0.50", "user": "admin", "jumphost": "bastion.example.com" }
  ]
}
.fi
.PP
Each entry has the following fields:
.TP
.B name
.br
Display name in the menu. Required and must be unique, since hosts are looked
up by it.
.TP
.B host
.br
Hostname or ip address. Required.
.TP
.B user
.br
Ssh user. Optional, defaults to the local user.
.TP
.B port
.br
Ssh port. Optional, defaults to 22, must be between 1 and 65535.
.TP
.B jumphost
.br
Bastion host to connect through with
.BR "ssh \-J" .
Optional.
.PP
.B port
and
.B jumphost
are stored as strings, and an omitted field and an empty string mean the same
thing.
.SH THE KITTY SSH KITTEN
Setting
.B "kitten_ssh"
to
.B true
makes connections go through kitty's ssh kitten instead of
.BR ssh .
It is a drop-in replacement that sets up shell integration on the remote
host, reuses existing connections to make reconnecting fast, and makes kitty's
terminfo database available there.
.PP
The kitten only works from inside a kitty terminal, so sshmgr falls back to
.B ssh
with a note when
.B kitten
is missing or sshmgr runs elsewhere. User, port and jumphost are passed
through unchanged.
.SS "If it prints \(aqConnecting to ...\(aq and then nothing happens"
A hang inside the ssh kitten, not in sshmgr, and it only happens when the
remote host asks for a
.BR password .
.PP
The kitten does not let
.B ssh
prompt in the terminal. Its askpass helper asks kitty to draw the prompt as an
overlay, by writing a
.B kitty-ask
escape sequence to the tty, and then waits for kitty to write the answer into a
shared memory segment. That wait is an endless poll with no timeout, so if kitty
never services the request nothing is printed and nothing is read, and the
connection sits there until interrupted.
.PP
Use key-based authentication where possible: no password is then ever
requested, the overlay is never needed, and the parts of the kitten that pay off
- near instant reconnects, remote shell integration, terminfo on the remote -
still work.
.PP
To keep typing passwords, let
.B ssh
prompt at the terminal instead, by putting
.PP
.RS
.nf
# ~/.config/kitty/ssh.conf
askpass ssh
.fi
.RE
.PP
in place. The rest of the kitten is unaffected; the only cost is a slightly
slower first connect. The same works for one connection with
.BI "kitten ssh --kitten askpass=ssh " host .
.PP
To see which path is in use, compare against plain
.BR ssh :
.PP
.RS
.nf
ssh \-o BatchMode=yes [user@]host
.fi
.RE
.PP
which fails immediately because no password is requested.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
.br
Used by
.BR "\-e" .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
.br
Leaves the add form without colour. The caret stays visible, since reverse
video is a text attribute rather than a colour.
.TP
.B KITTY_WINDOW_ID
.br
Set by kitty. Checked before
.B "kitten ssh"
is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.br
Success, including quitting a menu or form without changing anything.
.TP
.B 1
.br
The hosts file is not valid json, a host was not found, a port or name was
rejected, or the file could not be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sshmgr -a web-server example.com deploy 2222
Add a host without any questions.
.TP
.B sshmgr -a
Fill the form in and save it with ctrl-s.
.TP
.B sshmgr -a internal-db 10.0.0.50 admin 22 bastion.example.com
Add a host that is reached through a bastion.
.TP
.B sshmgr -r old-server test-box
Remove two hosts without opening the list.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sshmgr/known_hosts.json
The host list and the settings. Also created by
.B sshmgr \-e .
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.BR fzf ,
for the menus,
.BR jq ,
for reading and writing the file, and
.BR fping ,
optional, for
.B \-p
and the online status in the preview.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fzf (1),
.BR jq (1),
.BR fping (8),
.BR ssh (1)